::ENG2_U03_L05_Q01::[MLA.ENG2.R.08] Shared Information: The town can fund either bus shelters or trail repairs. The bus route serves 900 riders weekly. The trail has damaged sections. Argument A supports bus shelters. Question: Which evidence supports Argument A?{
~A. The trail has damaged sections.#Incorrect. This supports the opposing trail argument.
=B. The bus route serves 900 riders weekly.#Correct. This evidence directly supports the bus shelter claim.
~C. The town has a river.#Incorrect. This does not support bus shelters.
~D. The trail is outdoors.#Incorrect. This does not support the bus claim.
}



::ENG2_U03_L05_Q02::[MLA.ENG2.R.08] Shared Information: A school survey showed 62 percent wanted study space and 38 percent wanted public speaking activities. Argument A supports a study room. Argument B supports debate. Question: Which argument is more directly supported by the survey majority?{
~A. Argument B, because 38 percent is the majority.#Incorrect. 38 percent is not the majority.
~B. Neither argument mentions students.#Incorrect. Both arguments concern student needs.
~C. Argument B, because debate is always better.#Incorrect. This is preference, not evidence.
=D. Argument A, because 62 percent wanted study space.#Correct. The survey majority directly supports the study room.
}



::ENG2_U03_L05_Q03::[MLA.ENG2.R.08] Argument: "The school should buy new benches because one student said benches are nice." Question: What is the main weakness?{
=A. The evidence is too limited to support a schoolwide decision.#Correct. One student's opinion is weak evidence for a schoolwide purchase.
~B. The evidence is broad and measurable.#Incorrect. It is narrow and personal.
~C. The claim is missing completely.#Incorrect. The claim is to buy benches.
~D. The argument has too many statistics.#Incorrect. It has no statistics.
}



::ENG2_U03_L05_Q04::[MLA.ENG2.R.08] Shared Information: The gym roof leaks. The auditorium microphones fail. Argument A says fix the roof because leaks can damage the floor. Argument B says replace microphones because assemblies are hard to hear. Question: Which evaluation is strongest?{
~A. Argument A is stronger because roofs are more fun.#Incorrect. This is personal preference.
~B. Argument B has no evidence at all.#Incorrect. It uses microphone failures during assemblies as evidence.
=C. Argument A may be more urgent because it addresses safety and damage, while Argument B addresses communication.#Correct. This compares both arguments using evidence.
~D. Both arguments are about food.#Incorrect. Neither argument is about food.
}



::ENG2_U03_L05_Q05::[MLA.ENG2.R.08] Argument: "The trail should be repaired first because damaged sections create a safety risk for walkers and cyclists." Question: What is the claim?{
=A. The trail should be repaired first.#Correct. This is the position the author wants readers to accept.
~B. Walkers and cyclists exist.#Incorrect. This is a detail, not the claim.
~C. Safety risks can matter.#Incorrect. This supports the claim but is not the full claim.
~D. Damaged sections are words.#Incorrect. This is not an argument claim.
}



::ENG2_U03_L05_Q06::[MLA.ENG2.R.08] Shared Information: A town can either repair a playground or repaint the city sign. Argument A supports playground repair because broken equipment can injure children. Argument B supports repainting the sign because visitors see it first. Question: Which argument addresses safety more directly?{
~A. Argument B, because signs are colorful.#Incorrect. Color does not address safety.
~B. Neither argument mentions a reason.#Incorrect. Both include reasons.
~C. Argument B, because visitors exist.#Incorrect. Visitors seeing a sign is not safety evidence.
=D. Argument A, because broken equipment can injure children.#Correct. Injury risk is direct safety evidence.
}



::ENG2_U03_L05_Q07::[MLA.ENG2.R.08] Argument: "The city should build a new pool because my cousin likes swimming." Question: Why is this evidence weak?{
~A. It is too measurable.#Incorrect. It is not measurable.
=B. It is a personal example rather than broad public evidence.#Correct. A single cousin's preference is too narrow.
~C. It directly proves citywide need.#Incorrect. It does not prove citywide need.
~D. It includes too many sources.#Incorrect. It includes only one personal example.
}



::ENG2_U03_L05_Q08::[MLA.ENG2.R.08] Shared Information: The cafeteria can add a salad bar or replace broken water fountains. Argument A says add the salad bar because it expands lunch choices. Argument B says replace fountains because students need reliable water access. Question: Which statement fairly describes both claims?{
~A. Both claims ask for the same project.#Incorrect. They support different projects.
~B. Argument A has no claim.#Incorrect. Its claim is to add the salad bar.
=C. Argument A focuses on food choice, while Argument B focuses on water access.#Correct. This fairly identifies both positions.
~D. Argument B is about bus routes.#Incorrect. It is about water fountains.
}



::ENG2_U03_L05_Q09::[MLA.ENG2.R.08] Argument A uses the same attendance data to argue for later tutoring hours. Argument B uses the same data to argue for more online tutoring. Question: What should a reader compare?{
~A. Which author uses more colorful words only.#Incorrect. Word choice may matter, but the key issue is use of shared data.
~B. Which topic the reader likes more.#Incorrect. Preference is not analysis.
~C. Whether both arguments are poems.#Incorrect. They are arguments.
=D. How each author connects the same data to a different conclusion.#Correct. The task is to analyze use of shared information.
}



::ENG2_U03_L05_Q10::[MLA.ENG2.R.08] Argument: "The school should add a study room because 62 percent of surveyed students requested more study space." Question: Why is this evidence relevant?{
=A. It directly connects student need to the proposed study room.#Correct. The survey supports the claim.
~B. It is about weather.#Incorrect. The evidence is about student requests.
~C. It proves no students need debate.#Incorrect. It does not prove that no students need debate.
~D. It is unrelated to the claim.#Incorrect. It is directly related.
}



::ENG2_U03_L05_Q11::[MLA.ENG2.R.08] Shared Information: A park has broken lights and a damaged basketball court. Argument A says fix lights first for evening safety. Argument B says fix the court first for athletes. Question: Which evidence supports Argument A?{
~A. Athletes use the court.#Incorrect. This supports Argument B.
~B. The court is damaged.#Incorrect. This supports Argument B.
=C. Broken lights affect evening safety.#Correct. This supports fixing lights first.
~D. Basketball is a sport.#Incorrect. This does not support the light argument.
}



::ENG2_U03_L05_Q12::[MLA.ENG2.R.08] Argument: "The town should build more sidewalks because sidewalks help people walk away from traffic." Question: What is the reasoning?{
~A. The town should build more sidewalks.#Incorrect. That is the claim.
=B. Walking away from traffic improves pedestrian safety.#Correct. This explains why the evidence matters.
~C. Sidewalks exist.#Incorrect. This does not explain anything.
~D. People walk.#Incorrect. This is too general.
}



::ENG2_U03_L05_Q13::[MLA.ENG2.R.08] Argument A and Argument B both mention that the river trail is used by many ages. Argument A says repair the trail for exercise. Argument B says repair the trail for safe travel. Question: What is shared?{
=A. Both arguments use the same trail-use information.#Correct. They use the same information for related but different purposes.
~B. Both arguments discuss the bus route.#Incorrect. They discuss the trail.
~C. Both arguments reject evidence.#Incorrect. They use evidence.
~D. Both arguments are about school lunches.#Incorrect. They are about the trail.
}



::ENG2_U03_L05_Q14::[MLA.ENG2.R.08] Argument: "We should choose the debate club because public speaking builds confidence, even though more students requested study space." Question: What does the argument acknowledge?{
~A. It ignores all opposing information.#Incorrect. It mentions the opposing survey information.
~B. It changes the topic to roads.#Incorrect. It remains about school options.
~C. It has no claim.#Incorrect. The claim supports debate club.
=D. It acknowledges opposing evidence.#Correct. It admits more students requested study space.
}



::ENG2_U03_L05_Q15::[MLA.ENG2.R.08] Shared Information: A school can fund a garden or new library books. Argument A says the garden teaches science through hands-on work. Argument B says books support reading in every subject. Question: Which comparison is accurate?{
~A. Both arguments make the same claim.#Incorrect. They support different projects.
~B. Argument B is about gardening only.#Incorrect. It is about books.
=C. Argument A focuses on hands-on science, while Argument B focuses on schoolwide reading.#Correct. This fairly compares the reasoning.
~D. Argument A has no reasoning.#Incorrect. It reasons that gardens support hands-on science.
}



::ENG2_U03_L05_Q16::[MLA.ENG2.R.08] Argument: "The sound system should be replaced because clear sound helps students hear presentations." Question: What makes the evidence relevant?{
~A. It avoids the claim.#Incorrect. It directly supports the claim.
=B. It connects the proposed replacement to a communication need.#Correct. The evidence explains why sound matters.
~C. It discusses lunch choices.#Incorrect. Lunch is unrelated.
~D. It relies only on one friend's opinion.#Incorrect. The evidence focuses on presentation access.
}



::ENG2_U03_L05_Q17::[MLA.ENG2.R.08] Argument: "The town should repair the road because the road is old." Question: What information would make the argument stronger?{
~A. A statement that roads are outside.#Incorrect. That does not prove repair is needed.
~B. A joke about roads.#Incorrect. Humor does not strengthen evidence.
=C. Evidence showing the old road creates safety, access, or cost problems.#Correct. Age alone is weaker than specific impact.
~D. A sentence about unrelated school clubs.#Incorrect. That is irrelevant.
}



::ENG2_U03_L05_Q18::[MLA.ENG2.R.08] Shared Information: Both authors cite the same survey. Author A explains that the majority supports option A. Author B explains that a smaller group has a more urgent need. Question: What should readers evaluate?{
=A. Whether both explanations logically use the survey.#Correct. Readers should evaluate reasoning, not only the numbers.
~B. Which author has a shorter name.#Incorrect. That is irrelevant.
~C. Whether surveys are always false.#Incorrect. The task is to analyze use of the survey.
~D. Which option sounds fun.#Incorrect. Fun is not the validity test.
}



::ENG2_U03_L05_Q19::[MLA.ENG2.R.08] Argument: "The city should add crosswalk lights because three recent traffic reports show drivers often fail to stop at the school crossing." Question: What kind of support is used?{
~A. Unrelated description#Incorrect. The reports are related.
=B. Relevant evidence from reports#Correct. Traffic reports directly support the safety claim.
~C. Personal preference only#Incorrect. The support is based on reports.
~D. Fictional dialogue#Incorrect. No dialogue appears.
}



::ENG2_U03_L05_Q20::[MLA.ENG2.R.08] Argument A says repair the gym roof because leaks create safety risks. Argument B says replace microphones because assemblies are hard to hear. Question: Which detail would strengthen Argument B most?{
~A. A list of unrelated sports teams.#Incorrect. That does not support the sound system claim.
~B. A statement that roofs are above rooms.#Incorrect. This supports neither the sound system nor Argument B.
~C. A sentence saying microphones are shiny.#Incorrect. Appearance is irrelevant.
=D. Data showing how often microphone failures prevent students from hearing important information.#Correct. This would make the communication problem measurable and relevant.
}



::ENG2_U03_L05_Q21::[MLA.ENG2.R.08] Argument: "We should choose the option that helps more students, because school funds should meet the broadest need first." Question: What is this sentence mainly doing?{
=A. Providing reasoning that explains how to judge the evidence#Correct. It explains why the number of students helped matters.
~B. Giving a random topic#Incorrect. It explains a decision rule.
~C. Stating only a source title#Incorrect. It is reasoning.
~D. Avoiding the claim completely#Incorrect. It supports a claim.
}



::ENG2_U03_L05_Q22::[MLA.ENG2.R.08] Shared Information: The city can build a dog park or a playground. Argument A says dog owners requested space. Argument B says children need safe equipment. Question: Which question best tests validity?{
~A. Which word is longest?#Incorrect. Word length does not test validity.
~B. Which author uses the letter p?#Incorrect. That is irrelevant.
=C. Which option has more relevant evidence of public need and safety?#Correct. This evaluates evidence and reasoning.
~D. Which option sounds louder?#Incorrect. Loudness is not validity.
}



::ENG2_U03_L05_Q23::[MLA.ENG2.R.08] Argument: "The debate club should be funded because debate builds speaking confidence." Question: What would be the best opposing evidence?{
~A. A joke about microphones.#Incorrect. That is not evidence.
~B. A fact that classrooms have doors.#Incorrect. That does not oppose debate funding.
~C. A sentence saying debate exists.#Incorrect. That does not oppose the claim.
=D. Survey data showing most students requested quiet study space.#Correct. This directly supports an opposing option.
}



::ENG2_U03_L05_Q24::[MLA.ENG2.R.08] Argument A and Argument B use the same fact: "The trail is used by many ages." A says this supports exercise; B says this supports safe travel. Question: What does this show?{
~A. The trail is not mentioned.#Incorrect. The trail is central.
=B. The same information can support different arguments depending on reasoning.#Correct. Authors can connect shared facts to different purposes.
~C. Evidence can never be shared.#Incorrect. The same fact is shared here.
~D. Both authors must have the same claim.#Incorrect. They use the fact differently.
}



::ENG2_U03_L05_Q25::[MLA.ENG2.R.08] Argument: "The school should fix the gym roof first because leaks can damage the floor and create safety problems." Question: Which evaluation is best?{
=A. The argument is valid because the evidence is relevant and the reasoning explains urgency.#Correct. The evidence directly supports the claim and explains why it matters.
~B. The argument is invalid because roofs are boring.#Incorrect. Personal interest does not determine validity.
~C. The argument has no claim.#Incorrect. The claim is to fix the gym roof first.
~D. The argument is about debate clubs.#Incorrect. It is about the gym roof.
}
